<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        A kiosk (or client) is a venue PC running the Lanyard Client. It plays music, shows projection programs,
        drives DMX lighting and reads the laser game. <a href="/manage/clients">Clients</a> lists every kiosk and is
        Admin-only.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="The list">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Kiosk versions</strong> at the top counts how many PCs are on each version.</li>
            <li>Each kiosk shows its name, IP address, <strong>Version</strong> (and update ring), <strong>Last Login</strong>,
                <strong>Capabilities</strong> (Projection, DMX), <strong>Health</strong> and, while connected, how long
                it's been on.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Health">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Healthy</strong>: everything is fine.</li>
            <li><strong>Degraded</strong>: it's running, but something needs a look. Hover to see why: low disk space,
                high CPU or memory, the DMX device not open, the preferred audio device missing, zone scoreboard
                capture stopped, or offline fallback not ready.</li>
            <li><strong>Unresponsive</strong>: it has stopped sending heartbeats without closing properly (hung, crashed
                or network down).</li>
            <li><strong>Offline</strong>: unresponsive for long enough to count as down.</li>
            <li><strong>Disconnected</strong>: it closed properly, for example it was shut down.</li>
            <li><strong>No data</strong>: the client is too old to report health. It updates the next time it restarts.</li>
        </ul>
        <FluentText>
            During the location's opening hours, its Managers and the company's Admins are notified when a kiosk
            goes offline, when it's back, and when it stays degraded. People can turn these off under
            Notifications on their account page. The Kiosk Health dashboard widget shows the same thing.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Actions">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Settings</strong>: see the next tab.</li>
            <li><strong>Restart</strong>: choose <strong>Restart Lanyard Client</strong> (usually back within a few seconds)
                or <strong>Restart the whole PC</strong> (after a 10-second warning; can take a few minutes).</li>
            <li><strong>Diagnostics</strong>: fetch the kiosk's recent log for troubleshooting. Passwords and tokens are
                hidden.</li>
            <li><strong>Screens</strong>: <strong>Capture screens</strong> to see what each projected screen is showing right now.</li>
        </ul>
        <FluentText>
            Restart, Diagnostics and Screens only work while the kiosk is connected. Logs and screenshots are kept
            for 10 minutes and only Admins can see them.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="&quot;This display link isn't valid&quot;">
        <FluentText>
            A kiosk's projection screens only play programs the kiosk itself opened, so a screen's web address can't
            be copied and used on another computer, or for another company's program. If a projection screen shows
            <strong>This display link isn't valid</strong>, use <strong>Restart</strong> and choose
            <strong>Restart Lanyard Client</strong>, which opens the screens again.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
